1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:03,163
Aarav spots a small solar
lamp at the college gate.

2
00:00:03,463 --> 00:00:05,536
All day, it just sits in the sun.

3
00:00:05,836 --> 00:00:07,941
At night, it lights up by itself.

4
00:00:07,941 --> 00:00:09,290
How does it do that?

5
00:00:09,790 --> 00:00:12,488
The trick uses special
diodes that work with light.

6
00:00:12,788 --> 00:00:17,483
A diode is a one way gate for
current, made of p and n material.

7
00:00:17,783 --> 00:00:20,604
The first one turns
electricity into light.

8
00:00:20,604 --> 00:00:22,665
It is a light emitting diode.

9
00:00:22,965 --> 00:00:25,627
The second one turns light into a signal.

10
00:00:25,627 --> 00:00:27,006
It is a photodiode.

11
00:00:27,306 --> 00:00:29,685
The third one turns light into power.

12
00:00:29,685 --> 00:00:31,101
It is a solar cell.

13
00:00:31,701 --> 00:00:34,250
Let's start with the light emitting diode.

14
00:00:34,250 --> 00:00:35,975
Picture a playground slide.

15
00:00:36,275 --> 00:00:37,757
Electrons wait at the top.

16
00:00:37,757 --> 00:00:40,224
Holes, the empty seats,
wait at the bottom.

17
00:00:40,524 --> 00:00:44,843
When an electron slides down into a
hole, its extra energy comes out as

18
00:00:44,843 --> 00:00:45,743
light.

19
00:00:45,773 --> 00:00:49,588
On the breadboard: a cell,
a resistor and our diode.

20
00:00:49,938 --> 00:00:53,904
Forward bias means the plus side
of the cell meets the p side.

21
00:00:54,254 --> 00:00:55,162
Current flows.

22
00:00:55,162 --> 00:00:58,430
Electrons meet holes at the
junction, and the diode glows.

23
00:00:58,780 --> 00:01:00,221
Now flip the cell.

24
00:01:00,221 --> 00:01:01,818
This is reverse bias.

25
00:01:01,818 --> 00:01:03,518
No current, no light.

26
00:01:03,868 --> 00:01:07,536
So a light emitting diode
glows only in forward bias.

27
00:01:08,136 --> 00:01:09,958
How tall is the slide?

28
00:01:09,958 --> 00:01:12,808
That height is the energy gap, E g.

29
00:01:13,158 --> 00:01:15,466
Each flash of light is a photon.

30
00:01:15,466 --> 00:01:17,347
Its energy equals the gap.

31
00:01:18,047 --> 00:01:20,968
Photon energy is also h c over lambda.

32
00:01:20,968 --> 00:01:22,901
Lambda is the wavelength.

33
00:01:23,601 --> 00:01:26,556
So lambda equals h c divided by E g.

34
00:01:27,256 --> 00:01:32,107
Shortcut: lambda in nanometres equals
twelve forty divided by E g in electron

35
00:01:32,107 --> 00:01:33,007
volts.

36
00:01:33,426 --> 00:01:35,731
Watch the colour as the gap grows.

37
00:01:35,731 --> 00:01:38,376
One point eight electron volts gives red.

38
00:01:38,726 --> 00:01:40,592
Two point three gives green.

39
00:01:40,592 --> 00:01:41,912
Three gives violet.

40
00:01:42,262 --> 00:01:44,970
Bigger gap, shorter
wavelength, bluer light.

41
00:01:45,570 --> 00:01:49,925
For visible light, the gap must be at
least one point eight electron volts.

42
00:01:50,525 --> 00:01:52,367
These diodes are heavily doped.

43
00:01:52,367 --> 00:01:55,295
They use little power,
switch fast and last long.

44
00:01:55,895 --> 00:01:57,348
Now the second job.

45
00:01:57,348 --> 00:01:59,992
A photodiode turns light into a signal.

46
00:02:00,292 --> 00:02:02,619
Think of a door that light can knock open.

47
00:02:02,919 --> 00:02:05,026
We connect it in reverse bias.

48
00:02:05,026 --> 00:02:07,643
In the dark, almost no current flows.

49
00:02:07,993 --> 00:02:09,696
Now shine a little light.

50
00:02:09,696 --> 00:02:12,715
Each photon frees an
electron and leaves a hole.

51
00:02:13,065 --> 00:02:15,689
The junction field pulls the pair apart.

52
00:02:15,689 --> 00:02:17,252
A small current flows.

53
00:02:17,602 --> 00:02:19,900
More light, more pairs, more current.

54
00:02:19,900 --> 00:02:21,274
Watch the scope climb.

55
00:02:21,874 --> 00:02:23,097
Why reverse bias?

56
00:02:23,097 --> 00:02:25,408
In the dark, the current is tiny.

57
00:02:25,758 --> 00:02:28,556
So the extra current from
light is easy to see.

58
00:02:29,156 --> 00:02:33,618
The photon must carry at least E
g of energy, or nothing happens.

59
00:02:34,218 --> 00:02:37,363
Photodiodes sit inside remote
control receivers and light sensors.

60
00:02:37,713 --> 00:02:38,805
The third job.

61
00:02:38,805 --> 00:02:41,685
A solar cell turns light
into electric power.

62
00:02:41,985 --> 00:02:45,266
It is like a water wheel that
sunlight turns on its own.

63
00:02:45,566 --> 00:02:47,280
There is no battery here.

64
00:02:47,280 --> 00:02:49,289
The cell itself is the source.

65
00:02:49,639 --> 00:02:50,568
Three steps.

66
00:02:50,568 --> 00:02:53,356
Generation: light makes
electron hole pairs.

67
00:02:53,706 --> 00:02:57,042
Separation: the field at the
junction pushes them apart.

68
00:02:57,392 --> 00:03:01,473
Collection: the contacts gather them,
and current flows through the load.

69
00:03:02,073 --> 00:03:04,165
Its graph sits in the fourth quadrant.

70
00:03:04,515 --> 00:03:06,474
That means the cell gives power.

71
00:03:06,474 --> 00:03:07,848
It does not take it.

72
00:03:08,448 --> 00:03:13,423
With no load connected, we read
the open circuit voltage, V o c.

73
00:03:13,773 --> 00:03:17,706
With the leads shorted, we read
the short circuit current, I s c.

74
00:03:18,306 --> 00:03:21,875
The junction area is made
large, to catch more light.

75
00:03:22,475 --> 00:03:26,020
A gap near one point five
electron volts works best.

76
00:03:26,020 --> 00:03:28,290
Silicon has about one point one.

77
00:03:28,890 --> 00:03:31,052
Light out needs forward bias.

78
00:03:31,052 --> 00:03:33,160
Light in needs reverse bias.

79
00:03:33,160 --> 00:03:35,052
Power out needs no bias.

80
00:03:35,852 --> 00:03:37,095
Back to the lamp.

81
00:03:37,095 --> 00:03:40,100
By day, the solar cell
charges a small battery.

82
00:03:40,450 --> 00:03:42,669
At dusk, the cell's voltage falls.

83
00:03:42,669 --> 00:03:45,471
The circuit senses dark
and lights the diode.

84
00:03:46,071 --> 00:03:47,002
Worked example.

85
00:03:47,002 --> 00:03:50,842
A diode glows with light of wavelength
six hundred and twenty nanometres.

86
00:03:51,192 --> 00:03:52,839
What is its energy gap?

87
00:03:53,190 --> 00:03:56,847
E g equals twelve forty
divided by six twenty.

88
00:03:57,547 --> 00:03:59,410
That is two electron volts.

89
00:03:59,410 --> 00:04:03,291
In joules, three point two
times ten to the minus nineteen.

90
00:04:03,941 --> 00:04:05,403
Now, the exam trap.

91
00:04:05,753 --> 00:04:08,977
Many students think every
diode works in forward bias.

92
00:04:09,327 --> 00:04:10,227
Not these.

93
00:04:10,179 --> 00:04:12,489
A photodiode works in reverse bias.

94
00:04:12,489 --> 00:04:14,868
A solar cell needs no bias at all.

95
00:04:15,518 --> 00:04:19,824
And a solar cell has a wide
junction area, not a narrow one.

96
00:04:20,474 --> 00:04:21,557
Where is it asked?

97
00:04:21,557 --> 00:04:23,958
JEE Main asks statement
questions on all three.

98
00:04:24,308 --> 00:04:27,143
For example, in January
twenty twenty five.

99
00:04:27,793 --> 00:04:33,064
PUC and ISC board papers ask you to
explain each device with its diagram.

100
00:04:33,714 --> 00:04:34,614
Try this.

101
00:04:34,409 --> 00:04:38,244
Light of six hundred nanometres falls
on three photodiodes, with gaps one

102
00:04:38,244 --> 00:04:41,646
point eight, two point two and
two point six electron volts.

103
00:04:41,646 --> 00:04:42,953
Which give a current?

104
00:04:48,353 --> 00:04:49,253
A.

105
00:04:48,738 --> 00:04:51,539
Only the one point eight
electron volt diode.

106
00:04:51,889 --> 00:04:55,794
Photon energy is twelve
forty divided by six hundred.

107
00:04:55,794 --> 00:04:58,938
About two point zero seven electron volts.

108
00:04:59,288 --> 00:05:03,479
A current flows only if the photon
energy is more than the gap.

109
00:05:03,829 --> 00:05:06,822
Only one point eight is
below two point zero seven.

110
00:05:06,822 --> 00:05:08,538
So only that diode responds.

111
00:05:09,188 --> 00:05:10,735
Screenshot this card.

112
00:05:13,935 --> 00:05:15,889
Next, clippers and clampers.

113
00:05:15,889 --> 00:05:18,384
How can a diode cut or lift a wave?
